# Aithernet local environment.
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is git-ignored). The config loader auto-loads this file before
# resolving config, so you do NOT need `set -a; source .env; set +a`. Exported shell
# variables always override these values (override=False). Never commit real secrets.

# --- Coordinator: choose ONE provider ---
# provider + (executable for CLI) must match. Leave the model blank to use the provider's
# default model. Gemini reasons; Aithernet executes (the Gemini coordinator never runs
# tools, shells, files, or MCP itself).

# Option A — Gemini CLI (authenticated Google account; requires Node 20+ for gemini):
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_PROVIDER=gemini_cli
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_EXECUTABLE=gemini
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_MODEL=
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# Option B — OpenAI-compatible HTTP API (e.g. local Ollama). To use this instead, set
# AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_PROVIDER=openai_compatible and fill these in:
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_API_KEY=ollama
# AITHERNET_COORDINATOR_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b

# --- GNU Radio MCP (external gr-mcp launched over stdio) ---
# Point COMMAND at gr-mcp's own venv python and GR_MCP_DIR at the checkout (its cwd).
AITHERNET_GNURADIO_MCP_COMMAND=/home/aditya/gnu/aithernet/gr-mcp/.venv/bin/python
AITHERNET_GR_MCP_DIR=/home/aditya/gnu/aithernet/gr-mcp

# --- Coding agent (provider and executable MUST match) ---
# provider: claude_code  -> executable: claude
# provider: codex_cli    -> executable: codex
AITHERNET_CODING_AGENT_PROVIDER=codex_cli
AITHERNET_CODING_AGENT_EXECUTABLE=codex
AITHERNET_CODING_AGENT_WORKSPACE=/home/aditya/gnu/aithernet/workspace

# --- Autonomous mission execution (Stage 12) ---
# The autonomous worker loop and its budgets are configured in configs/node.yaml under the
# `mission_execution` block (enabled, worker_count, lease/poll intervals, default_budgets,
# context limits). Those are runaway-prevention limits, not autonomy levels. There are no
# secrets here. To run with only the manual one-step path, set mission_execution.enabled:
# false in configs/node.yaml.

# --- Authenticated agent transport (Stage 13A) ---
# Directory holding this node's Ed25519 identity key (OUTSIDE the repo). The private key is
# generated locally, stored with 0600 permissions, and never returned/printed/logged/committed.
# Defaults to ~/.local/state/aithernet/identity when unset. Use SEPARATE directories per node.
# AITHERNET_IDENTITY_STATE_DIR=/home/aditya/.local/state/aithernet/identity
# The rest of the transport (peers, outbox/inbox worker, timeouts, backoff, payload limits)
# is configured in configs/node.yaml under the `agent_transport` block. There are no secrets
# there. Peer trust is explicit: a new peer is untrusted until you run `aithernet peer trust`.

# --- Coordinator peer messaging & reply resumption (Stage 13B) ---
# Builds on the Stage 13A signed transport: the coordinator may send ONE message to a trusted
# peer mid-mission and durably wait for a correlated reply. There are NO secrets here. Two
# trust concepts stay separate: transport TRUST (pin a peer's Ed25519 key via `aithernet peer
# trust`) proves identity; application AUTHORIZATION (per-peer permissions) governs what an
# authenticated peer may do at the coordinator-message layer and defaults conservatively — a
# trusted peer is NOT auto-allowed to open coordinator requests. Grant that explicitly, e.g.
# `aithernet peer permissions set <peer-id> --may-send-requests --may-request-response`.
# The layer is tuned in configs/node.yaml under the `communication` block (enabled, text/data
# size + nesting bounds, default/max reply deadlines, timeout poll interval, inbound-request
# limits). Set communication.enabled: false there to disable peer messaging entirely. Untrusted
# remote text is always delimited/labeled in mission prompts, never executed as instructions.

# --- Distributed artifacts + canonical conversations (Stage 13D) ---
# Binary RF-artifact transfer between nodes uses a MANAGED content-addressed store on disk;
# bytes NEVER enter SQLite, events, prompts, or dashboard JSON. There are no secrets here. The
# store root holds objects/<sha256>, partial/, quarantine/, manifests/ and enforces path safety
# (no traversal/symlink/device), per-artifact + total + per-peer quotas, and a minimum-free-disk
# floor. Artifact transfer is OFF per peer by default — transport trust never authorizes a
# transfer; grant artifact permissions explicitly (separate from message permissions).
#   AITHERNET_ARTIFACT_STATE_DIR=/home/aditya/.local/state/aithernet/artifact-store
# All other artifact bounds (max_artifact_bytes, total_store_quota_bytes, per-peer quota,
# minimum_free_bytes, chunk_bytes, grant_ttl, retry ceiling, worker concurrency, and the
# remote-mission-status staleness window) live in configs/node.yaml under the `artifacts` block;
# there are no secrets there. Canonical-conversation resolution (a reply that omits
# conversation_id inherits its request/reply chain by provable correlation) needs no config.
# Stage 13D.2 completes cross-node transfer: a peer offers/requests an artifact, a SIGNED grant
# (bound to the exact transfer/digest/size/sender/receiver/expiry) authorizes ONE download, and
# the receiving node streams validated byte ranges over its authenticated /agent/v1/artifact
# endpoint, resumes from the persisted offset on interruption, verifies SHA-256 + size, and
# atomically imports. Grant a peer the SEPARATE artifact permissions (may_offer_artifacts,
# may_request_artifacts, may_receive_artifacts) explicitly — transport trust grants none.
# Stage 13D.3 adds authenticated remote mission-status synchronization: a node reports its OWN
# inbound-mission lifecycle to the requesting peer over the durable outbox, with infrastructure-
# allocated monotonic sequences, duplicate/older-update rejection, explicit unknown/fresh/stale/
# terminal freshness (status_freshness_seconds in configs/node.yaml), and a status-query path.
# Status carries NO prompts/tool internals/paths/bytes and creates NO MissionStep. Grant the
# SEPARATE mission-status permissions (may_publish_mission_status to accept a peer's status,
# may_receive_mission_status to send yours, may_query_mission_status to answer its queries)
# explicitly — they default OFF and are independent of trust, message, and artifact permissions.

# --- Operator dashboard (Stage 13C) ---
# The dashboard is a static SPA in web/ that talks to this node's API over CORS. It is
# configured entirely by ONE optional build-time variable (read by Vite from web/.env or the
# shell), NOT by this backend .env. There are no secrets. Point it at the node you want to
# observe; each browser tab targets exactly one node, so for a two-node setup build/serve one
# dashboard per node with its own base URL.
#   VITE_AITHERNET_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080   # default when unset
# The Stage 13C aggregate endpoints (/overview, /fleet, /conversations/{id}/timeline,
# /missions/{id}/distributed-timeline, /communications/summary, /reply-waits) and the operator
# composer (POST /communications/send) are always available on the backend; no extra backend
# configuration is required to enable them.

# --- Experimental Marconi RF backend (Stage 13A.5) ---
# Marconi is an EXTERNAL, experimental MCP backend (simulation/file-analysis; no live radio).
# It is opt-in and does NOT autostart — start it explicitly with `aithernet rf backend start
# marconi`. Aithernet talks to it only over its MCP subprocess; it never imports Marconi's
# Python modules and never runs Marconi's Claude Code plugin or skills. Leave these unset to
# keep Marconi disabled; the legacy GNU Radio backend remains the stable default either way.
# AITHERNET_MARCONI_COMMAND=/home/aditya/gnu/marconi/.venv/bin/marconi-mcp
# AITHERNET_MARCONI_DIR=/home/aditya/gnu/marconi
# AITHERNET_MARCONI_WORKSPACE=/home/aditya/.local/state/aithernet/rf/marconi

# --- Production deployment (Stage 14A) ---
# A production node keeps all persistent state beneath an explicit state root and never depends on
# the repository working directory. Set node_state_root in node.yaml (or generate a config with
# `aithernet node provision <root> --node-id <id> --node-name <name>`). Secrets are wired through
# the environment (this file / a systemd EnvironmentFile) and never written inline into node.yaml.
# Operational commands: `aithernet node preflight`, `aithernet health ready`, `aithernet backup
# create|verify|restore`, `aithernet upgrade preflight|apply|verify|rollback`, `aithernet
# diagnostics bundle`. See deploy/README.md. There are NO new secrets for Stage 14A.
# AITHERNET_NODE_STATE_ROOT=/srv/aithernet/node     # optional: wire node_state_root from env

# --- Managed SDR hardware inventory + leasing (Stage 14B) ---
# Managed hardware is OFF by default — a node with no SDR hardware stays fully usable for
# simulation, coding, coordination, and artifact analysis. There are NO new secrets here. When
# enabled (configs/node.yaml `hardware` block), the inventory worker runs configured discovery
# providers (soapy/uhd/static/static_file/command) on a bounded interval, persists FACTUAL devices/
# capabilities/health (unknown stays unknown; no device is ever invented), and the lease service
# grants durable, conflict-checked, bounded leases that authorize managed physical-device use. The
# coordinator may select only a KNOWN persisted device id + bounded purpose for an rf_device lease
# action — never a raw device string, driver argument, shell fragment, path, or environment. Lease
# renewal and inventory polling create no MissionStep. Operator lease acquire/release/revoke is
# gated by hardware.operator_lease_actions_enabled (default false; CLI/API/dashboard). Discovery
# providers, intervals, lease durations, required devices, and admin device->backend bindings are
# all configured in configs/node.yaml under the `hardware` block; the external RF backends
# (gr-mcp, marconi) are never modified. See deploy/README.md.

# --- Real SDR hardware qualification + RF execution (Stage 14C.1) ---
# The production soapy/uhd providers deep-probe a real device for FACTUAL capabilities, and an
# operator-invoked `aithernet hardware qualify run <device-id>` performs a real RX capture into the
# artifact store with full provenance. RF flowgraph execution runs in a SEPARATELY configured GNU
# Radio interpreter (configs/node.yaml hardware.qualification.gnuradio_python, default
# /usr/bin/python3) — Aithernet's own venv NEVER imports gnuradio, so the application and RF
# execution environments stay independently configurable. There are NO new secrets here; nothing
# transmits during qualification.

# --- Privacy-preserving telemetry, consent + data export (Stage 14E) ---
# Local-first data platform (configs/node.yaml `data_platform` block). The node is fully
# functional with it disabled. Export is OFF + PAUSED by default; TRAINING + RAW-ARTIFACT
# collection are OFF and require explicit RECORDED consent (an accepted participation policy +
# a per-category grant). Records are redacted + pseudonymized + sealed into immutable,
# compressed, optionally AES-256-GCM-encrypted batches before leaving the node, and delivered to
# a destination (local archive / authenticated HTTP ingestion / Google Drive / air-gapped).
# There are NO new secrets here — all credentials/keys are referenced by env-var NAME, never
# inlined or committed:
#   AITHERNET_DP_INGEST_SEED=<base64 Ed25519 seed>     # node->ingestion signing key (example name)
#   AITHERNET_DP_DRIVE_KEY=<base64 AES-256 key>         # Drive bundle encryption (example name)
#   AITHERNET_DP_DRIVE_OAUTH=/path/to/oauth_creds.json  # Drive OAuth credential path (outside Git)
#   AITHERNET_DP_PSEUDONYM_SECRET=<base64 256-bit>      # pseudonymization MASTER secret (example name)
# Pseudonymization is SECRET-backed (HKDF-SHA-256, tenant-separated): set
# data_platform.privacy.pseudonymization_key_ref in node.yaml to the env-var NAME above and put a
# high-entropy (>= 256-bit) base64 secret in that variable. The config + records store only the
# NAME + a non-secret key id, never the secret. Generate one OUTSIDE Git, e.g.
#   python -c "import os,base64;print(base64.b64encode(os.urandom(32)).decode())"
# When the secret is missing the platform FAILS CLOSED for pseudonymized/training/raw/external
# export (degraded data readiness) while local node + mission execution continue; it never falls
# back to a node fingerprint. NEVER commit generated secret material — this file holds only
# placeholders/references.
# A destination's `credential_ref` / `encryption_key_ref` / `oauth_credential_ref` hold the env
# var NAME (e.g. `AITHERNET_DP_INGEST_SEED`), and the value is read from the environment at use.
# Google Drive is OFF by default and is never the live DB, mission store, queue, or capture sink;
# real-account Drive verification is a manual operator command and never runs in automated tests.
# The standalone ingestion service is configured separately (see services/ingestion): INGEST_*.

# --- External-agent interoperability gateway (Stage 14D) ---
# OFF by default (configs/node.yaml `external_agents` block). Lets authenticated EXTERNAL
# (non-Aithernet) programs — orchestration systems, robotics, ground-station apps, local scripts —
# enroll an Ed25519 identity, submit idempotent missions, and receive durable SIGNED webhook /
# WebSocket callbacks with replay. It is SEPARATE from the node-to-node peer transport and never
# duplicates it. There are NO new secrets here: an operator provisions an agent's PUBLIC key
# (`aithernet external-agents create --public-key ...`); each request is signed (no secret on the
# wire); any optional bearer credential is stored hashed and shown once. Webhook + WebSocket
# delivery stay disabled until explicitly enabled, callbacks enforce an SSRF policy (https-only,
# DNS-rebinding-checked, no loopback/link-local/metadata/private destinations unless an operator
# allowlists a private CIDR), and notification/delivery activity creates NO MissionStep. See the
# reference client in examples/external_agent/ and deploy/README.md.

# --- Multi-node field operation + soak validation (Stage 14C.2) ---
# Operator-invoked validation of the node under repeated-operation, failure, and long-duration
# conditions (configs/node.yaml `field_validation` block). Read surfaces are always available;
# campaign/soak start, fault injection, and physical TX require explicit enablement (and confirmation
# for disruptive faults/TX). Fault injection is OFF by default and performs only bounded, predefined
# actions — never an arbitrary shell/network command. Sampling/lease-cycling/soak create no
# MissionStep, and no fake result is ever counted as physical evidence. There are NO new secrets.
